          <td><span class="postbody">The way I understand the resting system in WoW is this way:
<br />

<br />
It's set up so that you have to 'rest' (i.e. not fight) for about 8 hours real time to go from a total fatigue state to fully rested.  At fully rested you get about 200% of 'normal' exp from fighting, and it goes down in increments of 50% until it reaches 50% as you get more and more fatigued.  
<br />

<br />
So technically, you'll be getting twice the normal experience for fighting.  However, the problem is perception.  Anyone who plays these types of games knows that even though blizzard <span style="font-weight: bold">defines</span> fully rested as 200% exp, people are going to percieve that to be the norm, so anything less than that will be considered a penalty in most players' eyes.  They're basically trying to allow people with limited play time (since they're more likely to be be 'fully rested') more bang for their buck.  I think it's a good idea, but it will definitely have to be balanced.  
<br />

<br />
The key is how long it takes to get fatigued.  If you fight for 2 hours and go from fully rested to fully fatigued, then have to rest for 8 hours to get back to fully rested, then something's wrong.  I personally think they should target it at 8 hours of rest in a 24 hour period of play time should keep you at about middle of the road (i.e. 100% exp).  That way, if your character is rested for significantly more than 8 hours a day (which probably includes a lot on this board) you get a slight benefit for the time you are able to play, and if you're a super power gamer, then you will get slightly less out of your play time than the 'average' gamer.  I mean, if you're pissed because you can't play more than <span style="font-weight: bold">16 hours</span> in a day without suffering a penalty, then you need to get a life, or a job at least.
<br />

<br />
Of course all of this only applies to combat, exp from quests, crafting etc, suffers no effects from fatigue.</span><span class="gensmall"><i></i></span></td>
