        <tr>
          <td><span class="postbody">I think it's a noble idea, and it would work, but it's almost guaranteed to get a poor reception, so it probably won't fly.  The people who do play 18-20 hours a day (and they're not that uncommon) will think they're getting screwed.  Maybe they are and maybe they aren't, but it's a purely academic debate, since they'll make a lot of noise, and will get others to make a lot of noise too.  I think the idea is pretty much DOA, but maybe I'm wrong.
<br />

<br />
I think there are two simple things that any MMORPG can do to let the 'casual' gamer feel like they're actually getting their money's worth, and also letting the 'power' gamers not feel like they're getting the shaft:
<br />

<br />
 The first has been a problem since the dawn of MMORPGs, and that's downtime/overhead.  The problem with travel time is that if it takes an hour to get to where you're going to be playing, then somebody who plays only 2 hours a day already has 50% of their time lopped off right out of the gate, while somebody who plays 10 hours a day only has about 10% of their time dedicated to travel.  So travel time (and other forms of 'artificial' downtime) affect players with less play time disproportionately.  The problem with EQ (and virtually every other MMORPG) was that the designers somehow had the (flawed, in my view) notion that downtime equated to challenge, and thus fun.  Taking steps to reduce contrived downtime wouldn't make casual gamers feel like they're on a level playing field (because let's face it, more time invested is always going to equal faster progress, no matter what you do), but at least they can feel like they can log in for 2 hours and get something accomplished.
<br />

<br />
The other step should have been a no-brainer from the get-go, in my view.  That is to have players pay by the hour.  It could be set up similar to the way cellphones work.  A reasonable fee could be charged for those players who want unlimited access.  Say, 20$ a month.  For those who aren't able to play enough to make that worthwhile, they could purchase a lower cost plan with a limited number of hours (there could be several levels).  And if players choose to play more than their alloted time each month, the publisher could charge a premium for those extra hours.  For power gamers, nothing has changed.  They still get to pay a 15-20$ / month fee and play as much as they want.  For casual gamers, they may not be able to advance as fast as others, but at least they're not paying the same price for a service they'll be able to utilize only a fraction of the time that the other players do. It's win-win.</span><span class="gensmall"><i></i></span></td>
