          <td><span class="postbody"></span><table width="90%" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3" border="0" align="center"><tr> 	  <td><span class="genmed"><b>JonahD wrote:</b></span></td>	</tr>	<tr>	  <td class="quote">I think it's a noble idea, and it would work, but it's almost guaranteed to get a poor reception, so it probably won't fly.  The people who do play 18-20 hours a day (and they're not that uncommon) will think they're getting screwed.  Maybe they are and maybe they aren't, but it's a purely academic debate, since they'll make a lot of noise, and will get others to make a lot of noise too.  I think the idea is pretty much DOA, but maybe I'm wrong.</td>	</tr></table><span class="postbody">
<br />

<br />
I agree with you all the way on this.
<br />

<br />

<br />
</span><table width="90%" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3" border="0" align="center"><tr> 	  <td><span class="genmed"><b>JonahD wrote:</b></span></td>	</tr>	<tr>	  <td class="quote"> The first has been a problem since the dawn of MMORPGs, and that's downtime/overhead.  The problem with travel time is that if it takes an hour to get to where you're going to be playing, then somebody who plays only 2 hours a day already has 50% of their time lopped off right out of the gate, while somebody who plays 10 hours a day only has about 10% of their time dedicated to travel.  So travel time (and other forms of 'artificial' downtime) affect players with less play time disproportionately.  The problem with EQ (and virtually every other MMORPG) was that the designers somehow had the (flawed, in my view) notion that downtime equated to challenge, and thus fun.  Taking steps to reduce contrived downtime wouldn't make casual gamers feel like they're on a level playing field (because let's face it, more time invested is always going to equal faster progress, no matter what you do), but at least they can feel like they can log in for 2 hours and get something accomplished.</td>	</tr></table><span class="postbody">
<br />

<br />
where do i start....
<br />

<br />
*takes a deep breath*
<br />

<br />
I think this is HUGE, moreso now that I've been in both roles.  I feel that when I played HUGE amounts of time and was able to be online for 6 hours, I was rewarded for doing so, i.e. I would gain more levels and have more time to just goof around (running to qeynos to run around with arimis when he first started is a prime example).  Then the inevitable(sp) happened.....I got married and have since had children put in the mix.  So as it should be, my time is divided between work (8 hours) family (5-7 hours) and my time, which seems moreso than not to consist of sleep.  Which I haven't necessarily completely ruled out now that my time is divided, I might have to go back to my previous stance on the subject "Sleep is over-rated).  Now when I log in for my 1-2 hours every other day I can't:
<br />

<br />
-go to the bazaar to get new stuff
<br />
-do tradeskills
<br />
-run around goofing off with arimis
<br />
-roleplay (yes i used to love to rp in eq)
<br />
-questing is almost completely out of the question
<br />

<br />
It comes down to this.  Lets say I have 3 days I play 1-2 hours and I'm soloing with my bard, it looks something like this:
<br />

<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold">Day 1</span> - Run to zone, get set up in position I'm going to be hunting in.  Maybe kill a few mobs to test it out.  Log off
<br />

<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold">Day 2</span> - Grind out some exp for the entire duration of my time, leveling a couple times hopefully.
<br />

<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold">Day 3</span> - Run to town, pick up supplies, train skills, run to next experience spot, camp out.
<br />

<br />
Thats just an examply mind you, but alot of times that is what it comes down too.  Lets not throw in if I have to buy any equipment, thats a whole other day type thing.  So essentially the "growth" of my character (which would be gear and exp) takes a severe hit with this kind of system.
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
</span><table width="90%" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3" border="0" align="center"><tr> 	  <td><span class="genmed"><b>JonahD wrote:</b></span></td>	</tr>	<tr>	  <td class="quote">The other step should have been a no-brainer from the get-go, in my view.  That is to have players pay by the hour.  It could be set up similar to the way cellphones work.  A reasonable fee could be charged for those players who want unlimited access.  Say, 20$ a month.  For those who aren't able to play enough to make that worthwhile, they could purchase a lower cost plan with a limited number of hours (there could be several levels).  And if players choose to play more than their alloted time each month, the publisher could charge a premium for those extra hours.  For power gamers, nothing has changed.  They still get to pay a 15-20$ / month fee and play as much as they want.  For casual gamers, they may not be able to advance as fast as others, but at least they're not paying the same price for a service they'll be able to utilize only a fraction of the time that the other players do. It's win-win.</td>	</tr></table><span class="postbody">
<br />

<br />
From a business stand point it doesn't make any sense at all.  Only because its not mainline.  Meaning that no one has ever done it, and they still make LOTS of money.  No one wants to make less money than what is possible.  There are tons of "casual" gamers out there (me being one of them) who have accounts active that only play 6+ hours a week, and some (again, me being one of them) who have multiple accounts active for that 6+ hour week.  Arimis has 2 accounts active...one he never logs on and the other (his main account) hasn't been played in 2 months.  I'm sure there are another percentage of the eq world who do this.  It seems to me that I am rambling so  I'll get down to it.  
<br />

<br />
This could be a great idea for the consumer, but it won't be part of the MMORPG any time in the future.  Most cell phone companies use that "limited number of hours" to make their big bucks, cause they always undersell and over charge.  So instead of paying the 5 extra bucks to get the "unlimited" plan you pay 15 a month, and when you go over your minutes you end up paying 50 bucks.  Not a viable way of charging for a video game if you ask me.  I've worked for and been screwed by many cell phone companies who run stuff like this.  
<br />

<br />
I still love you Jonah!<br />_________________<br />[57 Luminary] Brrakk Attackk (Ogre) &lt;Guildless&gt; Zone:Sebilis &lt;7th Hammer&gt;
