          <td><span class="postbody"></span><table width="90%" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3" border="0" align="center"><tr> 	  <td><span class="genmed"><b>Leitbur wrote:</b></span></td>	</tr>	<tr>	  <td class="quote">
<br />
From a business stand point it doesn't make any sense at all.  Only because its not mainline.  Meaning that no one has ever done it, and they still make LOTS of money.  No one wants to make less money than what is possible.  There are tons of "casual" gamers out there (me being one of them) who have accounts active that only play 6+ hours a week, and some (again, me being one of them) who have multiple accounts active for that 6+ hour week.  Arimis has 2 accounts active...one he never logs on and the other (his main account) hasn't been played in 2 months.  I'm sure there are another percentage of the eq world who do this.  It seems to me that I am rambling so  I'll get down to it.  
<br />

<br />
This could be a great idea for the consumer, but it won't be part of the MMORPG any time in the future.  Most cell phone companies use that "limited number of hours" to make their big bucks, cause they always undersell and over charge.  So instead of paying the 5 extra bucks to get the "unlimited" plan you pay 15 a month, and when you go over your minutes you end up paying 50 bucks.  Not a viable way of charging for a video game if you ask me.  I've worked for and been screwed by many cell phone companies who run stuff like this.  
<br />
</td>	</tr></table><span class="postbody">
<br />

<br />
That's not entirely true.  It is true that for the moment developers can (and do) charge whatever they like for their service without worrying about customer complaints, since the gamers will just pay it and be done with it.  However, the reasoning behind the cellphone companies' billing scheme is that the customer is basically paying for the use of a resource.  A cellphone network has a limited amount of resources that can be used to process calls, and when they're gone, nobody else can make calls until they're freed up.  So obviously the company has to make money for the time that the resources are being used.  So when you buy a cellphone plan you're paying for X number of minutes in advance, but the company is hoping that you'll stay within those X minutes, because if you're not using your phone, then somebody else can be using theirs.  The fees for going over your minutes are basically just a deterrent to keep you within your alloted minutes.  A cell phone company would <span style="font-weight: bold">always</span> rather you switch to a higher (and more expensive) plan, rather than go over your minutes, because that's guaranteed money, as opposed to overages which are going to fluctuate.  That's also the reason why you get so many more night and weekend minutes with typical plans.  Those periods as a rule are very underutilized, so the company would like to get more people using their phones in those time periods, to maximize resource usage.
<br />

<br />
The same concept could apply to MMORPGs, albeit to a lesser extent.  The resources in this case would be bandwidth, and the same rules apply.  If you're not using bandwidth to play, then somebody else could be using it.  People with lower plans would tend to try and stick within their plan, thus freeing up resources on the off hours for others who want to play.  The real difference, I think, is that MMORPG customers seem to be willing to put up with a lot more crap than cell phone customers.  Take a look at lag, disconnects, bugs, etc.. None of that crap would be tolerated by the average cellphone customer.  Plus, you've got people willing to pay the same price as somebody else for something that they can use 10% as much.  If I had to pay the same price for 50 cellphone minutes as another customer pays for 500, I'd be finding another carrier.  Gamers don't seem to be willing to do that.  Would it eat into the bottom line of the gaming companies?  Probably, but come on.  I haven't seen any numbers, but I'd be willing to bet that the profit margin on a successful MMORPG is astronomical, otherwise there wouldn't be so many coming out.</span><span class="gensmall"><i></i></span></td>
